    tx: '<b>Transcription</b> is the copying of a gene from DNA into RNA in the nucleus. <b>RNA polymerase II</b> (orange, atomic structure PDB 5FLM), together with transcription factors, binds to the <b>promoter</b> (yellow zone, TATA box), unwinds about 14 base pairs and moves along the template strand 3′→5′, building RNA 5′→3′ by complementary base pairing (U instead of T). Above is the real structure of the yeast regulatory protein <b>GAL4</b> on its DNA site (PDB 1D66): this is how activators recognize their target genes by the sequence in the major groove. While the RNA is still being made, a <b>cap</b> (purple) is added to its 5′ end. After passing the polyadenylation signal, the polymerase cuts the RNA and leaves the DNA.',
